WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:03.294
In 2019, one in eleven people globally,

00:00:03.294 --> 00:00:05.463
were aged 65 and older.

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:07.882
By 2050, it'll be one in six.

00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:10.427
These shifting demographics,

00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:13.471
a combination of a long-term increase in life expectancy

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:15.515
and falling birth rates,

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:18.518
means the average person is getting older.

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:20.812
And while we should
celebrate living longer,

00:00:20.812 --> 00:00:24.149
we also have to accept that
means working longer, too.

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:25.567
We really need to think about

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:27.402
what this is going to look like

00:00:27.402 --> 00:00:30.030
when there's fewer younger
people and more older people

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.741
and how this will work moving forward.

00:00:32.741 --> 00:00:34.826
It could mean greater economic burden

00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:37.996
falling on the shrinking
group of working adults,

00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:39.831
but it could also be an opportunity

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:42.167
for individuals, as well as companies.

00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:46.671
The multi-generational
age-diverse workforce

00:00:46.671 --> 00:00:49.049
benefits in a whole range of ways.

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:52.135
Those policies that benefit older workers

00:00:52.135 --> 00:00:55.180
are highly likely to also benefit

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.432
younger and midlife workers.

00:00:57.432 --> 00:01:00.268
Society is having to
adapt to this new reality.

00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:03.063
The companies that realize
that age is just a number,

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:04.647
could reap the biggest rewards.

00:01:08.359 --> 00:01:09.778
My name is Christina Matz

00:01:09.778 --> 00:01:12.113
and I'm an associate
professor at Boston college,

00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:13.573
in the school of social work

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:17.327
and I study the aging
workforce and productive aging.

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:23.416
So when social security
was started back in 1965,

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:28.421
the expected life expectancy
was really around 70-75

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.757
and so people were expected to spend

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:32.675
a handful of years in retirement.

00:01:32.675 --> 00:01:35.887
And so now that we're
looking at, you know,

00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:39.516
15, 20, 30 years
potentially in retirement,

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:42.143
being able to sustain your income

00:01:42.143 --> 00:01:45.772
over the course of that period
of time, is a real issue.

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:48.233
So does that mean we have to
keep people in work longer,

00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:49.275
or do we need to rethink

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.361
the way we work, more broadly?

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:53.154
It's really important that we make sure

00:01:53.154 --> 00:01:56.282
that we shift away from this
focus on chronological age,

00:01:56.282 --> 00:02:01.287
more to capabilities, and
opportunities and strengths.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.375
There's still rampant amounts of ageism

00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:07.210
that exist in the workplace.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.379
We see it in the rates

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:12.006
of re-employment among unemployed workers.

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:13.800
We've actually seen an increase

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.386
in age discrimination
throughout the pandemic.

00:02:17.428 --> 00:02:18.263
In recent years,

00:02:18.263 --> 00:02:20.849
age discrimination has been
revealed beyond the anecdotal

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:23.059
and witnessed across the corporate level.

00:02:23.059 --> 00:02:24.477
Big companies have been accused

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.855
of laying off thousands of older employees

00:02:26.855 --> 00:02:30.191
to change their image, and
attract younger workers.

00:02:30.191 --> 00:02:32.819
A study for Deloitte suggested
that if you're older,

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:35.321
you're likely to be
considered less capable,

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:38.950
less able to adapt, or
less willing to pitch in.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.994
We found that individuals

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:43.246
who are on age diverse work teams,

00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:46.166
are more engaged than those

00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:48.626
who were on age-segregated work teams.

00:02:48.626 --> 00:02:51.588
And so really having that
diversity of opinion,

00:02:51.588 --> 00:02:53.798
that diversity of skills and talents,

00:02:53.798 --> 00:02:55.508
the diversity of experience,

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:57.218
really makes a huge difference

00:02:57.218 --> 00:02:59.053
in terms of engagement.

00:02:59.053 --> 00:03:00.597
Other studies have supported that,

00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:03.850
in terms of increasing
productivity, which in turn,

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:07.228
increases the likelihood of the employee

00:03:07.228 --> 00:03:08.605
staying with that organization.

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:10.273
So it reduces turnover.

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:13.401
Helping older people stay in work

00:03:13.401 --> 00:03:15.695
has wide-reaching benefits too.

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:17.655
According to a 2015 report,

00:03:17.655 --> 00:03:19.240
holding onto workers for just

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.326
three years beyond normal retirement,

00:03:21.326 --> 00:03:25.121
could add 55 billion pounds
to the British economy.

00:03:25.121 --> 00:03:27.874
It could also increase
wages and employment

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:29.626
for younger generations.

00:03:29.626 --> 00:03:31.544
If more older people stay in work,

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:33.963
they'll have more money
to spend in the economy.

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:37.967
But fixing the age demographic time bomb,

00:03:37.967 --> 00:03:39.302
as some like to put it,

00:03:39.302 --> 00:03:41.471
isn't just about delaying retirement.

00:03:41.471 --> 00:03:43.348
For many older workers,

00:03:43.348 --> 00:03:46.226
there's a simple reason to stop work,

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:48.228
our bodies make it hard to continue.

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:55.026
At BMW's Dingolfing plant
in southern Germany,

00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:57.612
the company had noticed
a time bomb of their own.

00:03:58.446 --> 00:04:02.367
The average age of the plant
workers in 2007 was 39,

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:06.246
and was due to rise to 47 by 2017.

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:09.249
Former head of the powertrain
plant, Nikolaus Bauer,

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:12.293
was concerned about what that
would mean for productivity.

00:04:12.293 --> 00:04:15.463
If you bear in mind that older population

00:04:15.463 --> 00:04:18.925
tend to need longer time
to cure from a sickness,

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:20.843
have longer absenteeism,

00:04:20.843 --> 00:04:23.888
have maybe physical abilities

00:04:23.888 --> 00:04:27.558
which are not as good
as from young workers.

00:04:27.558 --> 00:04:28.601
What can we do?

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:32.063
We have to improve the competitiveness

00:04:32.063 --> 00:04:33.690
of the existing factory.

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:35.692
The plant had committed

00:04:35.692 --> 00:04:38.444
to guaranteeing employment
to its loyal workers,

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:41.072
rather than forcing them
into early retirement.

00:04:41.072 --> 00:04:42.991
So it used the situation to learn

00:04:42.991 --> 00:04:46.369
about its workforce
and improve conditions.

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:49.872
We created a facility within the plant

00:04:49.872 --> 00:04:54.419
and installed the age average

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:56.921
that the other plant
will have in 10 years.

00:04:56.921 --> 00:05:00.300
Meaning, we took elder people,
brought them to the line,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.926
and we took younger people out.

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:05.471
Line managers work with
the employees to identify

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:08.975
what might help them work
better and in more comfort.

00:05:10.184 --> 00:05:11.811
They identified 70 measures

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:14.105
which they put in place for a small cost,

00:05:14.105 --> 00:05:16.107
such as changing footwear,

00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:18.943
installing wooden flooring
and adjustable chairs,

00:05:18.943 --> 00:05:20.862
and adding variety to the stations

00:05:20.862 --> 00:05:23.031
the employees worked
at throughout the day.

00:05:24.198 --> 00:05:28.411
It turned out that eight
hours on one workstation

00:05:28.411 --> 00:05:30.663
is physically very, very demanding.

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:32.749
We should have a rotation concept.

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:37.754
First, something demanding
for shoulders and arms,

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:42.675
then let's say something
for, demanding for the legs.

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:45.928
Then let's say something
demanding for the brain.

00:05:45.928 --> 00:05:47.680
They were not in principle,

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.142
dedicated to improve the productivity,

00:05:51.142 --> 00:05:54.437
they were dedicated to improve ergonomics,

00:05:54.437 --> 00:05:58.441
but ergonomics, measures of ergonomic,

00:05:58.441 --> 00:06:01.736
also turned out to be very
helpful for productivity.

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:06.074
Within three months,
productivity was up 7%.

00:06:06.074 --> 00:06:08.951
The line's absenteeism rate was halved,

00:06:08.951 --> 00:06:10.870
and the defect rate dropped to zero.

00:06:11.996 --> 00:06:12.830
All the others

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:14.832
which are not dedicated to that line said,

00:06:14.832 --> 00:06:18.044
"that is the pensioners line," at first.

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:22.048
But the pensioners
line proved to be better

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:24.092
than all the other lines within one year.

00:06:24.967 --> 00:06:27.512
So why do you think the
older workers did better?

00:06:27.512 --> 00:06:29.514
Age brings experience.

00:06:29.514 --> 00:06:32.934
If you are old, you have
already made enough faults,

00:06:34.268 --> 00:06:36.396
which you won't ever repeat.

00:06:36.396 --> 00:06:38.106
Ten years later, I can say,

00:06:38.106 --> 00:06:42.110
this is more or less,
basic in every BMW factory.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:47.240
According to HSBC,

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.867
15% fewer adults will join the workforce

00:06:49.867 --> 00:06:52.036
within the next two decades.

00:06:52.036 --> 00:06:53.371
Thanks to coronavirus,

00:06:53.371 --> 00:06:56.791
the global baby drought could
be even more pronounced.

00:06:56.791 --> 00:06:59.252
Harnessing the contributions
of older workers

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:02.296
could become a serious
competitive advantage.

00:07:02.296 --> 00:07:03.673
This idea has already birthed

00:07:03.673 --> 00:07:06.134
a new career path: The 'Returnship'.

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:09.512
So a 'returnship' is really

00:07:09.512 --> 00:07:12.181
sort of like a student internship,

00:07:12.181 --> 00:07:14.976
except it's tweaked for the
mid-career professional.

00:07:14.976 --> 00:07:18.354
It is a short-term work opportunity,

00:07:18.354 --> 00:07:21.023
where the company and the individual

00:07:21.023 --> 00:07:23.317
have the opportunity to test out

00:07:23.317 --> 00:07:24.902
the working relationship,

00:07:24.902 --> 00:07:27.780
before the employer makes
the hiring decision.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.533
Carol Fishman Cohen co-founded iRelaunch,

00:07:30.533 --> 00:07:33.744
a company specializing in
the career re-entry space.

00:07:33.744 --> 00:07:35.913
We're working with people
who are returning to work

00:07:35.913 --> 00:07:38.749
after career breaks, for
a whole range of reasons

00:07:38.749 --> 00:07:40.585
and they could be returning

00:07:40.585 --> 00:07:42.879
in their 40s, 50s or 60s.

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:47.008
Sometimes people are returning
to work after retirement,

00:07:47.008 --> 00:07:50.303
so they're un-retiring because retirement

00:07:50.303 --> 00:07:52.388
didn't turn out to be what
they thought it was gonna be.

00:07:52.388 --> 00:07:54.515
Or maybe they have financial pressure

00:07:54.515 --> 00:07:56.601
that they weren't expecting.

00:07:56.601 --> 00:07:59.020
What are employers looking
for from relaunchers?

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:02.023
If you think about the qualities

00:08:02.023 --> 00:08:04.025
of the relauncher population,

00:08:04.025 --> 00:08:06.819
they're educated, they
have great work experience,

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:09.489
they have a mature perspective.

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:11.699
They have worked before,

00:08:11.699 --> 00:08:13.826
they've worked on
multi-generational teams,

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:15.786
they've worked with
different personalities,

00:08:15.786 --> 00:08:17.413
they've faced work deadlines.

00:08:17.413 --> 00:08:19.624
You don't have to teach that to them.

00:08:19.624 --> 00:08:21.292
Our work lives are getting longer.

00:08:21.292 --> 00:08:24.170
Some of us could spend five
or six decades in work.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:25.379
So should we all start

00:08:25.379 --> 00:08:27.798
thinking differently about our careers?

00:08:27.798 --> 00:08:29.926
The career break can really be a gift

00:08:29.926 --> 00:08:31.969
and I don't think people realize that,

00:08:31.969 --> 00:08:33.846
but once you're on career break,

00:08:33.846 --> 00:08:35.806
it's the first time many of us

00:08:35.806 --> 00:08:38.226
allow ourselves to step back and reflect

00:08:38.226 --> 00:08:39.602
on whether we were even on

00:08:39.602 --> 00:08:41.729
the right career path to begin with.

00:08:41.729 --> 00:08:43.314
Because if you think about it,

00:08:43.314 --> 00:08:45.650
some of us just fell into a career

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:48.152
without really knowing that much about it.

00:08:48.152 --> 00:08:49.862
It was our first job out of school,

00:08:49.862 --> 00:08:51.697
we took another job in that same area,

00:08:51.697 --> 00:08:54.408
and all of a sudden we had a career.

00:08:54.408 --> 00:08:56.536
And others of us were maybe

00:08:56.536 --> 00:08:58.287
fulfilling other people's expectations,

00:08:58.287 --> 00:09:02.792
like maybe our parents wanted us
to go into a certain field

00:09:02.792 --> 00:09:05.253
and we were feeling some
pressure, and so we did that.

00:09:05.253 --> 00:09:07.338
And that's one of the attributes

00:09:07.338 --> 00:09:09.632
of people who relaunch their careers,

00:09:09.632 --> 00:09:12.093
is they have a more mature perspective,

00:09:12.093 --> 00:09:14.512
they know themselves a lot better,

00:09:14.512 --> 00:09:15.805
and they have a better sense

00:09:15.805 --> 00:09:20.059
of where they can add the
most value to an employer.

00:09:20.059 --> 00:09:21.602
When you think progressive workplace,

00:09:21.602 --> 00:09:23.020
you'd be forgiven for not thinking

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.481
of financial services right away,

00:09:25.481 --> 00:09:28.526
but it's where the
returnship actually started.

00:09:28.526 --> 00:09:29.860
It took off throughout Wall Street,

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:31.279
as the banking industry realized

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:33.239
it was losing successful women

00:09:33.239 --> 00:09:35.533
and its senior roles were thinning out.

00:09:35.533 --> 00:09:39.287
They're intentionally bringing
back people who are older,

00:09:39.287 --> 00:09:41.789
because they want them to fill

00:09:41.789 --> 00:09:43.749
those mid to senior level roles,

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:45.793
where they haven't had enough women

00:09:45.793 --> 00:09:48.170
because of attrition over time.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:50.464
It almost goes counter to what

00:09:50.464 --> 00:09:51.966
you might be thinking about

00:09:51.966 --> 00:09:54.302
with ageism in the workforce,

00:09:54.302 --> 00:09:57.138
because most of the people who get hired

00:09:57.138 --> 00:09:58.431
through return to work programs

00:09:58.431 --> 00:10:01.017
are in their 40s
or 50s or older.

00:10:03.227 --> 00:10:05.313
While many might dream of retiring early,

00:10:05.313 --> 00:10:08.149
for most it's far from reality.

00:10:08.149 --> 00:10:08.983
In the U.S.

00:10:08.983 --> 00:10:12.069
it's estimated to cost a million
dollars to retire at age 65

00:10:12.069 --> 00:10:16.157
yet, 21% of Americans have no savings.

00:10:16.157 --> 00:10:19.410
Research also shows that people
who stop working and retire,

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:22.455
lose social contacts,
and suffer ill health.

00:10:22.455 --> 00:10:24.123
Employment on the other hand,

00:10:24.123 --> 00:10:26.459
brings both physical and
mental health benefits,

00:10:26.459 --> 00:10:30.296
and staves off the time we
draw on savings, and pensions.

00:10:30.296 --> 00:10:31.714
We really need to think about

00:10:31.714 --> 00:10:33.716
alternative ways of working.

00:10:33.716 --> 00:10:36.677
More flexible working arrangements,

00:10:36.677 --> 00:10:41.057
that allow people to phase
out of retirement slowly,

00:10:41.057 --> 00:10:43.309
over a longer period of time.

00:10:43.309 --> 00:10:46.562
Considering things like,
mid-life sabbaticals.

00:10:46.562 --> 00:10:51.067
Considering education
that is more lifelong

00:10:51.067 --> 00:10:53.194
and extended across the lifespan.

00:10:53.194 --> 00:10:54.862
We have to be careful when we talk

00:10:54.862 --> 00:10:58.282
about solutions around working longer,

00:10:58.282 --> 00:11:00.076
to be sure that people are not

00:11:00.076 --> 00:11:02.328
pushed out of work when
they really need it

00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:04.705
and pushed out of health insurance

00:11:04.705 --> 00:11:07.166
and other benefits that come with working,

00:11:07.166 --> 00:11:09.335
that older adults often need.

